"Composition of Permutation Representations of Triangle Groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.00721)\nciteturn3academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.3 years ago; Authors: Siddiqua Mazhar ... $$ We examine a method for composition of permutation representations of a triangle group $\\Delta(p,q,r)$ that was used in Everitt's proof of Higman's 1968 conjecture that every Fuchsian group has amongst its homomorphic images all but finitely many alternating groups.\nTitle: Composition of Permutation Representations of Triangle Groups\nAuthors: Siddiqua Mazhar\nDate: Wed Aug  2 12:33:11 2017\n\nA triangle group is denoted by $\\Delta(p,q,r)$ and has finite presentation $$ \\Delta(p,q,r)=\\langle x,y | x^p=y^q=(xy)^r=1 \\rangle .$$ We examine a method for composition of permutation representations of a triangle group $\\Delta(p,q,r)$ that was used in Everitt's proof of Higman's 1968 conjecture that every Fuchsian group has amongst its homomorphic images all but finitely many alternating groups. We see that some of these compositions must give imprimitive representations, and in particular situations, where the representations being composed are all equivalent copies of an alternating group in the same degree, we can give a complete description of the structure of the composition of the representations. This article contains the main results of the author's PhD thesis.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComposition of permutation representations of triangle groups: Communications in Algebra: Vol 48, No 2 (https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/00927872.2019.1662911)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.1 years ago; Crawled: last week; Siddiqua Mazhar School of Mathematics, Statistics and Physics, Newcastle University, Newcastle upon Tyne, UK; ;Department of Mathematics and Social Sciences, Sukkur Institute of Business Administration University, Sukkur, Pakistan Correspondence mazharsiddiqua@gmail.com ... We examine a method for composition of permutation representations of a triangle group $\\Delta (p,q,r)$ that was used in Everitt’s proof of Higman’s 1968 conjecture that every Fuchsian group has amongst its homomorphic images all but finitely many alternating groups.\n\nCommunications in Algebra Volume 48, 2020 - Issue 2\n\nSubmit an article Journal homepage\n\n131\n\nViews\n\n1\n\nCrossRef citations to date\n\n0\n\nAltmetric\n\nOriginal Articles\n\n# Composition of permutation representations of triangle groups\n\nSiddiqua MazharSchool of Mathematics, Statistics and Physics, Newcastle University, Newcastle upon Tyne, UK; ;Department of Mathematics and Social Sciences, Sukkur Institute of Business Administration University, Sukkur, Pakistan Correspondence mazharsiddiqua@gmail.com\n\nPages 792-802 | Received 13 Jan 2018, Accepted 28 Jul 2019, Published online: 17 Sep 2019\n\n## Abstract\n\nA triangle group is denoted by $\\Delta (p,q,r)$ and has finite presentation\n\n$$\\Delta (p,q,r)=〈x,y|{x}^{p}={y}^{q}={(\\mathit{xy})}^{r}=1〉.$$\n\nWe examine a method for composition of permutation representations of a triangle group $\\Delta (p,q,r)$ that was used in Everitt’s proof of Higman’s 1968 conjecture that every Fuchsian group has amongst its homomorphic images all but finitely many alternating groups. We see that some of these compositions must give imprimitive representations. In particular situations, where the representations being composed are all equivalent copies of an alternating group in the same degree containing at least one handle, then $G=\\Delta (p,q,r)$ has a quotient ${C}_{p}^{\\text{deg}-1}⋊{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\text{deg}}$. We also prove that if G has a quotient ${\\mathcal{A}}_{\\text{deg}}$ containing at least two handles, then G has a quotient ${\\mathcal{A}}_{m}\\wr {\\mathcal{A}}_{\\text{deg}}$ for many $m\\in \\mathbb{Z}$. This article contains the main results of the author’s PhD thesis.\n\nKeywords:\n\n  * Coset diagram\n  * imprimitive group\n  * triangle groups\n\nMATHEMATICS SUBJECT CLASSIFICATIONS:\n\n  * 20XX\n  * 05C25\n\n### Acknowledgements\n\nMy deepest gratitude to my supervisor Prof. Sarah Rees and Prof. Derek Holt for their constant guidance during my PhD.\n\n## Additional information\n\n### Funding\n\nThis research is supported by Faculty for the Future, Schlumberger Foundation.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComposition of permutation (https://theses.ncl.ac.uk/jspui/bitstream/10443/3857/1/Mazhar%20S%202017.pdf)\nciteturn3search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; The idea for composition is the following: if we have p coset diagrams of degrees\ndeg1, deg2, .., degp for the triangle group G, each having k-handles then we can join ... permutation representations π1, . . . , πt on distinct, disjoint, ﬁnite sets Ω1, . . . , Ωt,\nChapter 3. Composition\nb\na\nb\na\n(a) 1-handle\n(b) 2-handle\nb\na\n(c) 3-handle\nThe idea for composition is the following: if we have p coset diagrams of degrees\ndeg1, deg2, .., degp for the triangle group G, each having k-handles then we can join\nthem together by using these handles to ﬁnd a coset diagram of G of some larger\ndegree.\nSuppose that t is an integer with t ≤p. Then we deﬁne U (or Ut if it is necessary\nto specify t) to be the disjoint union of Ω1, Ω2, . . . , Ωt. We shall use this notation\nthroughout the remainder of the thesis. The following proof of the theorem is a new\n(algebraic) proof of Proposition 3.1 in [6].\nTheorem 3.2. Suppose that for some t ≤p.\nIf G = ∆(p, q, r) has transitive\npermutation representations π1, . . . , πt on distinct, disjoint, ﬁnite sets Ω1, . . . , Ωt,\nand that [a1, b1], . . . , [ap, bp] are all k-handles, with [ai, bi] a handle in Ωji. If ji = ji′\nfor i ̸= i′, then suppose that the handles [ai, bi] and [ai′, bi′] are disjoint. Now deﬁne\npermutations φx, φy of U = Ut via\nφx\n=\nπ1(x) · · · πt(x) ◦(a1, · · · , ap)(bb, bp−1, · · · , b1),\nφy\n=\nπ1(y) · · · πt(y).\n36--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSome Remarks on Combinatorial Wall-Crossing | The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/view/v29i1p36)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.7 years ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; We also present a conjecture on combinatorial wall-crossing which was found using computer experiments.\n\n# Some Remarks on Combinatorial Wall-Crossing\n\n  * Galyna Dobrovolska\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.37236/10479\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe establish a new simple explicit description of the Young diagram at each step of the combinatorial wall-crossing algorithm for the rational Cherednik algebra applied to the trivial representation. In this way we provide a short and explicit proof of a key theorem of P. Dimakis and G. Yue. We also present a conjecture on combinatorial wall-crossing which was found using computer experiments.\n\nPublished\n\n2022-02-25\n\nHow to Cite\n\nDobrovolska, G. (2022). Some Remarks on Combinatorial Wall-Crossing. The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, 29(1), #P1.36. https://doi.org/10.37236/10479\n\nIssue\n\nVolume 29, Issue 1 (2022)\n\nArticle Number\n\nP1.36--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPartitioning the vertices of a torus into isomorphic (https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/scott/Papers/toruspacking.pdf)\nciteturn3search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; Therefore, if there were to exist a perfect H-packing of Gn for some n, (forgetting ap- ... We have also shown that when k is odd and not a prime power, the base conditions are not\nrest of H is determined. In particular, the only coordinates in the embedding of H which\nvary are those which vary on the embedding of the cycles. In particular, the coordinates\ncorresponding to Zn remain ﬁxed in the embedding of H.\nTherefore, if there were to exist a perfect H-packing of Gn for some n, (forgetting ap-\npropriate edges) we get a packing of Cn\nk with the H from Theorem 1.5. However, such a\npacking cannot exist (as shown by Theorem 1.5).\nIn this paper we have determined that for any even k the base conditions on H are suﬃ-\ncient to guarantee a perfect induced H-packing of Cn\nk for n suﬃciently large (Theorem 1.4).\nWe have also shown that when k is odd and not a prime power, the base conditions are not\nsuﬃcient (Theorem 1.5). Thus it is natural to wonder what happens when k is an odd prime\npower.\nConjecture 5.2. Let k be an odd prime power and m ≥1. Suppose that H is an induced\nsubgraph of Cm\nk such that |V (H)| divides km. Then there exists n0 such that, for all n ≥n0,\nCn\nk admits a perfect induced H-packing.\nIn Section 4 we considered the question of whether, for a given graph H, there exists d\nsuch that it is possible to cover the edges of Qd with edge-disjoint copies of H. We showed\nthat the necessary conditions that H is a subgraph of Qd and that |E(H)| divides |E(Qd)| are\nnot suﬃcient. When H is a path and suitable divisibility conditions hold, Erde [2] and Anick\nand Ramras [1] independently showed that there is a partition of E(Qn) into edge-disjoint\ncopies of H. It would be interesting to prove analogous results for other graphs.\nQuestion 5.3. For which graphs G does there exist some d such that the edges of Qd can\nbe partitioned into edge disjoint copies of d?1\nNaturally, in order for this to be possible we require that |E(G)| divides |E(Qd)| and\nthat it is possible to express d −1 as the sum of degrees of vertices in G. For example, it\nseems likely that this should be suﬃcient when G is an even cycle.\nAcknowledgement. The authors would like to thank the anonymous referees for their very\nhelpful comments.\nReferences\n[1] D. Anick and M. Ramras, Edge decompositions of hypercubes by paths, Australas. J.\nCombin. 61 (2015), 210–226.\n[2] J. Erde, Decomposing the cube into paths, Discrete Math. 336 (2014), 41–45.\n[3] The Math Forum, Two tiling problems, http://mathforum.org/kb/message.jspa?\nmessageID=6223965, retrieved on 7 November 2016.\n1We remark that this question diﬀers slightly from the published version of this paper.\n16--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRecent Advances in Graphical Regular Representations of Groups Binzhou Xia University of Melbourne 26 Sep 2023, Online Binzhou Xia (UoM) | Graphical regular representations | 26 Sep 2023, Online 1 / 20 (https://www.gtseminar.xyz/documents/2023-09-26-bzx.pdf)\nciteturn3search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Spiga’s conjectures If Cay(G, S) is a cubic GRR of G, then S either consists of three involutions or contains exactly one involution. Conjecture (Spiga 2017 Commun.\nSpiga’s conjectures If Cay(G, S) is a cubic GRR of G, then S either consists of three involutions or contains exactly one involution. Conjecture (Spiga 2017 Commun. Algebra) Binzhou Xia (UoM) | Graphical regular representations | 26 Sep 2023, Online 14 / 20\n--- | --- | -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMIT Open Access Articles (https://dspace.mit.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/8ddd48c8-f738-4b5b-b8f6-663de4c58c18/content)\nciteturn3search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; In this paper, we deﬁne the combinatorial wall-crossing transformation and ... Two Equivalent Deﬁnitions of Mullineux Transpose ... Example of n = 5 and a General Conjecture\nCOMBINATORIAL WALL-CROSSING AND THE MULLINEUX\nINVOLUTION\nPANAGIOTIS DIMAKIS AND GUANGYI YUE\nAbstract. In this paper, we deﬁne the combinatorial wall-crossing transformation and\nthe generalized column regularization on partitions and prove that a certain composition of\nthese two transformations has the same eﬀect on the one-row partition (n). As corollaries\nwe explicitly describe the quotients of the partitions which arise in this process. We also\nprove that the one-row partition is the unique partition that stays regular at any step of the\nwall-crossing transformation.\nContents\n1.\nIntroduction\n1\n2.\nPreliminaries\n2\n2.1.\nTwo Equivalent Deﬁnitions of Mullineux Transpose\n4\n2.2.\nColumn Regularization\n7\n3.\nTwo Series of Transformations and the Main Theorem\n7\n4.\nDetailed Descriptions of the Sequence of Partitions\n15\n5.\nUniqueness of Monotonicity\n17\nAppendix A.\nExample of n = 5 and a General Conjecture\n18\nReferences\n20\n1. Introduction\nWall-crossing functors appear in the context of inﬁnite-dimensional representations of com-\nplex semisimple Lie algebras, and Beilinson and Ginzburg studied its relation with transla-\ntion functors in [BG99]. More recently, wall-crossing functors have appeared in the study of\nquantized symplectic resolutions of singularities as perverse equivalences between diﬀerent\ncategories of modules, for more details one can look at [ABM11,BL17,Los15b]. These per-\nverse equivalences induce bijections between irreducible objects in the corresponding derived\ncategories, which are referred to as the combinatorial wall-crossing. In the classical case of\nLie algebra representations they are related to the cactus group actions [HKRW17,Los15a].\nIt is called combinatorial wall-crossing because in case of rational Cherednik algebras of type\nA, the derived categories are parametrized by rational numbers, and the bijection among two\ncategories parametrized by consecutive rational numbers (with denominator bounded above)\nis like crossing a wall. Our work is motivated by combinatorial wall-crossing for representa-\ntions of rational Cherednik algebras in large positive characteristic, where the combinatorial\n(P. Dimakis) Department of Mathematics, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, 94305, USA\n(G. Yue) Department of Mathematics, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge,\nMA, 02139, USA\nE-mail addresses: (P. Dimakis) pdimakis12345@gmail.com, (G. Yue) gyyue@mit.edu.\n1----------------------------------------------------------------